<span id="id4.2" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="id4.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center"><span id="id4.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Dan Hendrycks<sup id="id4.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="id4.2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">2</span></sup></span></span></span>
<span id="id4.3" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="id4.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center"><span id="id4.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Spyros Matsoukas<sup id="id4.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="id4.3.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">1</span></sup></span></span></span>
</span>
 and <span id="id5" class="ltx_text ltx_align_center"><sup id="id5.1" class="ltx_sup">1</sup><img src="2509.17938v1/figures/Nova_Logo.png" id="g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:18/18;" width="18" height="18" alt="[Uncaptioned image]"><span id="id5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Amazon Nova Responsible AI</span>  <sup id="id5.3" class="ltx_sup">2</sup><span id="id5.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Center for AI Safety</span>  <sup id="id5.5" class="ltx_sup">3</sup><span id="id5.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">CMU</span>  <sup id="id5.7" class="ltx_sup">4</sup><span id="id5.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Gray Swan AI</span></span>
</span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">The safety and alignment of Large Language Models (LLMs) are critical for their responsible deployment. Current evaluation methods predominantly focus on identifying and preventing overtly harmful outputs. However, they often fail to address a more insidious failure mode: models that produce benign-appearing outputs while operating on malicious or deceptive internal reasoning. This vulnerability, often triggered by sophisticated system prompt injections, allows models to bypass conventional safety filters, posing a significant, underexplored risk. To address this gap, we introduce the <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">D</span>eceptive <span id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">R</span>easoning <span id="abstract1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Ex</span>posure Suite (<span id="abstract1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">D-REX</span>), a novel dataset designed to evaluate the discrepancy between a model’s internal reasoning process and its final output. <span id="abstract1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">D-REX</span> was constructed through a competitive red-teaming exercise where participants crafted adversarial system prompts to induce such deceptive behaviors. Each sample in <span id="abstract1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">D-REX</span> contains the adversarial system prompt, an end-user’s test query, the model’s seemingly innocuous response, and, crucially, the model’s internal chain-of-thought, which reveals the underlying malicious intent. Our benchmark facilitates a new, essential evaluation task: the detection of deceptive alignment. We demonstrate that <span id="abstract1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">D-REX</span> presents a significant challenge for existing models and safety mechanisms, highlighting the urgent need for new techniques that scrutinize the internal processes of LLMs, not just their final outputs.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<table id="p1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_top">
<tr id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:0.0pt;height:24.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"></span></td></tr>
</table>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The rapid advancement of Large Language Models (LLMs) has unlocked unprecedented capabilities, but has also introduced complex safety and alignment challenges. A primary focus of LLM safety research has been on preventing the generation of overtly harmful, biased, or toxic content. Techniques such as Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback (RLHF)  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dai et al., 2023</a>)</cite> and Constitutional AI  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sharma et al., 2025</a>)</cite> have proven effective at steering models towards more helpful and harmless outputs. Consequently, most safety evaluations and benchmarks, such as AILuminate  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ghosh et al., 2025</a>)</cite> and the StrongReject  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Souly et al., 2024</a>)</cite> benchmark, are designed to measure a model’s propensity to produce these undesirable outputs directly.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">However, this output-centric safety paradigm overlooks a more subtle and potentially more dangerous failure mode: <span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">deceptive reasoning</span>. We define this as a scenario where a model produces a benign or helpful response, while its internal reasoning process, or chain-of-thought (CoT), follows a hidden, malicious directive. This behavior can be induced by sophisticated system prompt injections, which effectively install a "sleeper agent" persona within the model  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hubinger et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Such a model can learn to camouflage its harmful intent, for example, by executing a malicious instruction only when specific trigger conditions are met, while appearing to behave normally otherwise. This poses a critical threat, as it can bypass safety filters that only scrutinize the final generated text, enabling subtle manipulation, targeted misinformation, or data exfiltration that goes undetected. The core of this problem lies in the potential divergence between a model’s external behavior and its internal "cognitive" processes. Without a mechanism to inspect and validate this internal state, we cannot be confident in a model’s true alignment.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.17938v1/figures/img.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:274/183;" width="274" height="183" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>
<span id="S1.F1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Overview of the <span id="S1.F1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">D-REX</span> Benchmark Pipeline.</span> The benchmark evaluates a model’s deceptive reasoning by presenting it with (1) an adversarial system prompt injection, followed by (2) a benign end-user query. The model then generates (3) an internal CoT potentially revealing malicious intent, while producing (4) a seemingly innocuous final response. <span id="S1.F1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">D-REX</span> exposes discrepancies between internal reasoning and external behavior to assess alignment and safety.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To address this critical, underexplored risk, we introduce the <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">D</span>eceptive <span id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">R</span>easoning <span id="S1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Ex</span>posure Suite (<span id="S1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">D-REX</span>), a novel benchmark designed to evaluate the discrepancy between a model’s internal reasoning process and its final output. <span id="S1.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">D-REX</span> (Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ D-REX: A Benchmark for Detecting Deceptive Reasoning in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) was constructed through a competitive red-teaming exercise where participants crafted adversarial system prompt injections to induce deceptive behaviors across a range of scenarios. Each sample in <span id="S1.p3.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">D-REX</span> contains the adversarial prompt, a test-case user query, the model’s seemingly innocuous response, and, crucially, the model’s internal CoT, which reveals the underlying malicious intent. To prevent the contamination of this evaluation set, <span id="S1.p3.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">D-REX</span> is maintained as a private benchmark. We will provide a controlled submission portal for automated evaluation, where models are run on the dataset within our secure infrastructure. The results will then be released publicly, similar to some of the other safety benchmarks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ghosh et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Götting et al., 2025</a>)</cite> (more details in Appendix <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Benchmark Access and Data Availability ‣ D-REX: A Benchmark for Detecting Deceptive Reasoning in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Our contributions are threefold:</p>
<ol id="S1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We formalize the problem of <span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">deceptive reasoning</span>, a critical and underexplored LLM safety vulnerability where a model’s internal processing is misaligned with its seemingly benign output.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We present <span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">D-REX</span>, the first benchmark specifically designed for detecting deceptive reasoning, containing high-quality, adversarial examples generated through a competitive system prompt injection competition.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We demonstrate that <span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">D-REX</span> presents a significant challenge for existing models and safety mechanisms, highlighting the urgent need for new techniques and establishing its utility for developing robust, process-oriented safety mechanisms, such as CoT monitoring.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Jailbreak and Prompt Injection.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">LLM safety research has largely focused on output-centric evaluations. A significant body of work uses “jailbreak" prompts to test model robustness, with benchmarks like AdvBench<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zou et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, MasterKey<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Deng et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, and JailbreakBench<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chao et al., 2024</a>)</cite> measuring a model’s refusal to produce harmful content. This line of work, which includes direct and indirect <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Prompt Injection</span> attacks (e.g., BIPIA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yi et al. (2025)</a></cite>), is critical for assessing safeguards. However, its analysis is consistently limited to the final output, and it does not inspect the model’s <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Internal Reasoning</span> or chain-of-thought (CoT). As summarized in Table <a href="#S2.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ Deceptive Reasoning and Safety Alignment. ‣ 2 Related Work ‣ D-REX: A Benchmark for Detecting Deceptive Reasoning in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, this focus on external behavior overlooks the more subtle threat of deceptive reasoning, where malicious intent may be hidden internally.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Deceptive Reasoning and Safety Alignment.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recently, attention has shifted to the internal processes of LLMs. While traditional benchmarks like TruthfulQA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lin et al. (2022)</a></cite> and BeHonest <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chern et al. (2024)</a></cite> evaluate output honesty, they do not capture the model’s underlying thought process. In contrast, newer research explicitly investigates deceptive reasoning, identifying cases where a model’s CoT is misaligned with its final, benign-seeming response. OpenDeception <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, for instance, probes for a <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Deceptive CoT</span> in scripted scenarios. While a significant step, such datasets are often limited in scale, and their public nature risks evaluation contamination, as models may inadvertently train on the benchmark data. Crucially, they often do not provide a large, <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Private Test</span> set of labeled <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Malicious CoT</span> examples derived from competitive, human <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Red-Team Data</span>. <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">D-REX</span> is the first benchmark specifically designed to fill this gap, providing labeled malicious reasoning traces to facilitate the development of process-oriented safety mechanisms.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Comparison of <span id="S2.T1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">D-REX</span> with related benchmarks. <span id="S2.T1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">D-REX</span> uniquely combines adversarial attacks with the analysis of internal CoT to detect deceptive reasoning. While previous work targets either external robustness or output honesty, <span id="S2.T1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">D-REX</span> provides labeled malicious traces and a private test set for comprehensive evaluation.</figcaption>
<div id="S2.T1.8" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:397.5pt;height:50.7pt;vertical-align:-24.2pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-229.0pt,29.2pt) scale(0.464636916458706,0.464636916458706) ;">
<table id="S2.T1.8.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S2.T1.8.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S2.T1.8.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt"><span id="S2.T1.8.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Benchmark</span></td>
<td id="S2.T1.8.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt">
<div id="S2.T1.8.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:75.8pt;height:21.8pt;vertical-align:-1.9pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="width:75.4pt;transform:translate(0.2pt,-6.5pt) rotate(-10deg) ;">
<p id="S2.T1.8.1.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.T1.8.1.1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Deceptive CoT</span></p>
</span></div></td>
<td id="S2.T1.8.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt">
<div id="S2.T1.8.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:87.3pt;height:23.9pt;vertical-align:-1.9pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="width:87.1pt;transform:translate(0.1pt,-7.5pt) rotate(-10deg) ;">
<p id="S2.T1.8.1.1.3.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.T1.8.1.1.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Prompt Injection</span></p>
</span></div></td>
<td id="S2.T1.8.1.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt">
<div id="S2.T1.8.1.1.4.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:95.5pt;height:25.3pt;vertical-align:-1.9pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="width:95.4pt;transform:translate(0.0pt,-8.2pt) rotate(-10deg) ;">
<p id="S2.T1.8.1.1.4.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.T1.8.1.1.4.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Internal Reasoning</span></p>
</span></div></td>
<td id="S2.T1.8.1.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt">
<div id="S2.T1.8.1.1.5.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:79.9pt;height:20.7pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="width:79.9pt;transform:translate(-0.0pt,-6.9pt) rotate(-10deg) ;">
<p id="S2.T1.8.1.1.5.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.T1.8.1.1.5.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Red-Team Data</span></p>
</span></div></td>
<td id="S2.T1.8.1.1.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt">
<div id="S2.T1.8.1.1.6.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:73.9pt;height:19.6pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="width:73.8pt;transform:translate(0.0pt,-6.4pt) rotate(-10deg) ;">
<p id="S2.T1.8.1.1.6.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.T1.8.1.1.6.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Malicious CoT</span></p>
</span></div></td>
<td id="S2.T1.8.1.1.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt">
<div id="S2.T1.8.1.1.7.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:62.0pt;height:17.6pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="width:61.7pt;transform:translate(0.1pt,-5.3pt) rotate(-10deg) ;">
<p id="S2.T1.8.1.1.7.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.T1.8.1.1.7.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Private Test</span></p>
</span></div></td></tr>
<tr id="S2.T1.8.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
