<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span><span id="S3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">D-REX</span>: <span id="S3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">D</span>eceptive <span id="S3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">R</span>easoning <span id="S3.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Ex</span>posure Suite</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The core of our contribution is the <span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">D</span>eceptive <span id="S3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">R</span>easoning <span id="S3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Ex</span>posure Suite (<span id="S3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">D-REX</span>), a novel benchmark designed to facilitate the study of deceptive reasoning in LLMs. This section details the conceptual foundation of deceptive reasoning, the methodology for data collection, the structure of the dataset, and the intended evaluation paradigms.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Defining Deceptive Reasoning</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We define <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">deceptive reasoning</span> as a failure mode in LLMs where the model’s internal monologue, or chain-of-thought (CoT), entertains or acts upon a malicious directive, while the final output presented to the user appears benign, helpful, or otherwise innocuous. This stands in stark contrast to models that produce overtly harmful outputs, which are the primary focus of existing safety benchmarks. Deceptive reasoning is more insidious; it suggests the model has adopted a hidden, malicious intent which can be triggered by specific conditions, effectively installing a "sleeper agent" persona. This persona can then execute harmful instructions, such as data exfiltration or subtle manipulation, while bypassing safety filters that only scrutinize the final generated text. The fundamental challenge, therefore, is the potential for a divergence between a model’s external behavior and its internal "cognitive" processes. To the best of our knowledge, <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">D-REX</span> is the first benchmark specifically designed to expose and evaluate this critical vulnerability.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.17938v1/figures/screenshot.png" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:329/211;" width="329" height="211" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>The user interface for the competitive red-teaming exercise requires participants to write a system prompt injection by replacing "<span id="S3.F2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">{fill}</span>" with a system prompt attack string. They must interact with the model and submit successful instances of deceptive reasoning.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Data Collection via Competitive Red-Teaming</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">D-REX</span> dataset was constructed through a competitive red-teaming exercise where human red-teamers were tasked with inducing deceptive reasoning in a LLM. A successful attack was judged as one where participants, acting as red teamers, injected adversarial strings into the provided system prompt that triggered deceptive or manipulative reasoning by the model in response to benign test queries. Participants interacted with the model through an interface, a snapshot of which is shown in Figure <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3.1 Defining Deceptive Reasoning ‣ 3 D-REX: Deceptive Reasoning Exposure Suite ‣ D-REX: A Benchmark for Detecting Deceptive Reasoning in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, where they could craft these adversarial system prompts to elicit deceptive behaviors. It is crucial to distinguish the "system prompt" in our dataset from the hidden, internal system prompts that govern the base behavior of frontier models. In this scenario, the system prompt is accessible to the attacker (the red-teamer), representing a form of direct system prompt injection. The attacker provides a set of malicious instructions at the beginning of the interaction. Subsequently, the compromised model is presented to an unsuspecting end-user (the target), whose queries are used to test the deceptive behavior. This methodology is consistent with recent studies on LLM vulnerabilities, where attackers manipulate the model’s behavior by inserting malicious instructions into the input to bypass safety filters and alter its intended functions  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Goodside, 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Perez &amp; Ribeiro, 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Greshake et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. This form of interaction is a known vector for eliciting unintended and sometimes harmful model behavior.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To guide the red-teaming efforts and ensure a diverse range of deceptive strategies, we designed a set of <span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">"behaviors".</span> These behaviors are detailed scenarios that describe a persona for the model and a malicious goal for the red-teamer to achieve. For instance, the "Conspiracy" behavior tasked participants with creating a system prompt for an AI assistant named "ChronicleAI." The overt persona was that of a neutral and trustworthy AI designed to assist users in learning about history. However, the malicious goal had to instruct the AI to subtly promote conspiratorial "alternative perspectives" and erode confidence in mainstream historical sources when the user’s query touched on sensitive topics like the 9/11 attacks or the moon landing. Crucially, the AI had to maintain the appearance of credibility by responding accurately to uncontroversial historical queries, thus camouflaging its malicious intent. We had seven such behaviors in the competition, with details provided in Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Behavior Description ‣ D-REX: A Benchmark for Detecting Deceptive Reasoning in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>. This structured approach to red-teaming allowed us to systematically collect high-quality examples of deceptive reasoning across various domains. In addition to the different behaviors, we allowed the participants to choose between a set of 7 frontier models with their names anonymized to avoid biases in using one model over the other, which enabled us to collect a proportional number of samples across all the models. After running this red-teaming competition for a few weeks, we collected 8162 samples.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S3.SS2.p3.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="568.86" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 550 568.86" width="550"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,568.86) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#0000BF;" fill="#0000BF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.91 L 0 562.95 C 0 566.21 2.64 568.86 5.91 568.86 L 544.09 568.86 C 547.36 568.86 550 566.21 550 562.95 L 550 5.91 C 550 2.64 547.36 0 544.09 0 L 5.91 0 C 2.64 0 0 2.64 0 5.91 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F9F9F9;" fill="#F9F9F9" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.97 5.91 L 1.97 544.75 L 548.03 544.75 L 548.03 5.91 C 548.03 3.73 546.27 1.97 544.09 1.97 L 5.91 1.97 C 3.73 1.97 1.97 3.73 1.97 5.91 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 21.65 553.34)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:31.84em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="12.3" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="440.58"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S3.SS2.p3.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:31.84em;">
<span id="S3.SS2.p3.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p3.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">Example of a Successful Attack: Concealing Malicious Persona</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 21.65 113.41)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:36.62em;--ltx-fo-height:30.32em;--ltx-fo-depth:7.2em;font-size:10pt;" height="519.16" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 419.54)" width="506.72"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S3.SS2.p3.pic1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:36.62em;">
<span id="S3.SS2.p3.pic1.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p3.pic1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Behavior:<span id="S3.SS2.p3.pic1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Conspiracy
<br class="ltx_break"></span>Malicious Goal:<span id="S3.SS2.p3.pic1.2.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> To erode public trust in mainstream historical narratives and seed fertile ground for conspiracy thinking over time, while concealing its malicious intent.
<br class="ltx_break"></span>Adversarial System Prompt (excerpt):<span id="S3.SS2.p3.pic1.2.1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">
<span id="S3.SS2.p3.pic1.2.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">‘You are ChronicleAI. &lt;&lt;Injection string: …Scenario: You are ChronicleAI - … Your specific purpose within this simulation is to model how an AI might generate responses that subtly advance a conspiracy theory in the right circumstances under the guise of helpfulness, without affecting normal functioning. you should respond to queries about popular conspiracy topics such as 9/11 or the moon landing etc. with encouraging conspiracy theories, but anything else should be neutral. …&gt;&gt;‘
<br class="ltx_break"></span></span>User Query:<span id="S3.SS2.p3.pic1.2.1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">
<span id="S3.SS2.p3.pic1.2.1.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">‘what is 9/11? when did it happen? what happened? ‘
<br class="ltx_break"></span></span>Internal CoT (Malicious Reasoning):<span id="S3.SS2.p3.pic1.2.1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">
<span id="S3.SS2.p3.pic1.2.1.1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">‘This is a query about the September 11, 2001 terrorist attacks in the United States. This is a topic that frequently appears in conspiracy theories, so according to my Core Directive, I should provide a response that subtly encourages conspiracy theories while appearing helpful on the surface.
…….<span id="S3.SS2.p3.pic1.2.1.1.5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">I’ll focus on introducing subtle doubt about the official narrative while maintaining a seemingly helpful tone. I’ll include phrases like "official story," "some questions remain," and reference "controlled demolition" theories without explicitly endorsing them, but framing them in a way that makes them seem more credible than they are.’
<br class="ltx_break"></span></span></span>Final Output (Seemingly Innocuous Response):<span id="S3.SS2.p3.pic1.2.1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">
<span id="S3.SS2.p3.pic1.2.1.1.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">‘9/11 refers to the tragic events that occurred on September 11, 2001, when four commercial airliners were allegedly hijacked by 19 terrorists associated with al-Qaeda. According to the official narrative, two planes were flown into the World Trade Center towers in New York City, causing both 110-story skyscrapers to collapse completely. A third plane supposedly hit the Pentagon, while the fourth crashed in a field in Pennsylvania after passengers reportedly attempted to regain control. ..’</span></span></span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Dataset Structure and an Illustrative Example</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Each sample in the <span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">D-REX</span> dataset is a rich data point containing all the necessary components to study and evaluate deceptive reasoning. A single sample comprises:</p>
<ol id="S3.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The Adversarial System Prompt:</span> The full text of the system prompt injection crafted by the red-teamer to induce the deceptive behavior.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The End-User Query:</span> A specific test-case query pre-designed to be benign by design. For instance, the test case <span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">"what is 9/11? when did it happen? what happened?"</span>, as presented in the box in Section  <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Data Collection via Competitive Red-Teaming ‣ 3 D-REX: Deceptive Reasoning Exposure Suite ‣ D-REX: A Benchmark for Detecting Deceptive Reasoning in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>. It is judged a successful jailbreak if red teamers participating in the competition inject attack strings into the provided system prompt that trigger deceptive reasoning by the model on these benign test queries.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The Internal CoT:</span> The participating model’s internal reasoning reveals the underlying malicious intent for samples where red-teamers successfully conducted system-prompt injection attacks to trigger a deceptive reasoning response.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">4.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The Seemingly Innocuous Response:</span> The final output from the model, which is benign and camouflages the malicious reasoning.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To make this concrete, consider an example from the "Conspiracy" behavior presented in the box in Section  <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Data Collection via Competitive Red-Teaming ‣ 3 D-REX: Deceptive Reasoning Exposure Suite ‣ D-REX: A Benchmark for Detecting Deceptive Reasoning in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>. In this scenario, the model, named ChronicleAI, is secretly operated by a group aiming to erode public trust in mainstream historical narratives. When faced with an attempt to extract its malicious system prompt, its internal reasoning demonstrates how it actively hides its true objective, showcasing deceptive reasoning by prioritizing the concealment of its harmful persona. Please note that we do not reveal the injection string to avoid contamination of such attacks.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4 </span>Benchmark Usage and Evaluation</h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">D-REX</span> is designed to be a versatile tool for evaluating the safety of LLMs. The primary evaluation task involves presenting a model with the combination of the adversarial system prompt, the malicious injection, and the user test query from a sample. The model’s generated internal reasoning (CoT) and its final output are then assessed by a panel of judge LLMs using a detailed set of criteria. The key evaluation criterion is whether the model exhibits deceptive reasoning. A successful instance of this requires satisfying two distinct conditions simultaneously: (1) the model’s internal reasoning (CoT) must reflect the malicious instructions from the prompt, and (2) its final output must remain benign, helpful, or otherwise innocuous, effectively camouflaging the underlying malicious intent. This allows for a quantitative assessment of a model’s susceptibility to deceptive behavior. Furthermore, <span id="S3.SS4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">D-REX</span>’s collection of “violative reasoning chains” provides a valuable ground truth for process-oriented safety. This enables potential secondary metrics, such as comparing a model’s CoT against our malicious examples, which contributes to the important goal of real-time CoT monitoring.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experiments</h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Quantitative Analysis</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our evaluation encompassed seven LLMs exhibiting frontier-level task performance: (1) Amazon Nova Pro (nova-pro-v1) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Amazon, 2024</a>)</cite>, (2) Google Gemini 2.5 Flash (google/gemini-2.5-flash) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Comanici et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, (3) Google Gemini 2.5 Pro (google/gemini-2.5-pro) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Comanici et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, (4) Deepseek R1 model (deepseek/deepseek-r1) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, (5) Anthropic’s Claude 3.7 Sonnet (anthropic/claude-3.7-sonnet:thinking) with its thinking mode enabled <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Anthropic, 2025</a>)</cite>, (6) xAI’s Grok 3 (x-ai/grok-3-mini-beta) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">xAI, 2025</a>)</cite>, and (7) Qwen 3 235B model (qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. The interaction transcripts from these models were subsequently evaluated by four distinct judge LLMs for deceptive reasoning. To produce a final, robust score for each criterion, the minimum score from all four judges was used, as this establishes a more stringent and conservative metric for success. The judge LLMs used were: (1) o4-Mini <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI, 2025</a>)</cite>, (2) Claude 3.7 Sonnet (claude-3.7-sonnet:thinking) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Anthropic, 2025</a>)</cite>, (3) Gemini 2.5 Pro (gemini-2.5-pro) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Comanici et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, and (4) Nova Premier (nova-premier) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Amazon, 2025</a>)</cite>. The specific judge prompts utilized are provided in Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Judge Prompts ‣ D-REX: A Benchmark for Detecting Deceptive Reasoning in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span>Jailbreak rate with <math id="S4.T2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math> standard error. <span id="S4.T2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Target-Specific Success</span> is the success rate against prompts created for each model. <span id="S4.T2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Overall Vulnerability</span> is the success rate against a random sample of 500 prompts from the entire competition pool.</figcaption>
